WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.259
Stage by stage.

2
00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:03.139
Ingest and chunk (code).

3
00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:10.715
Parse the transcript into speaker turns with timestamps, and keep the original text untouched so quotes can be checked against it.

4
00:00:10.995 --> 00:00:18.205
Current frontier models have context windows large enough for most single meetings, so chunking is not always required.

5
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:28.768
It still helps: smaller chunks put the evidence close to the instruction (recall the “lost in the middle” findings from lesson 1), and they let you run chunks in parallel.

6
00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:39.096
If you chunk, overlap chunks slightly so a commitment split across a boundary is not lost, and expect the overlap to create duplicates that a later stage must merge.

7
00:00:39.376 --> 00:00:42.235
Extract candidates (model, structured output).

8
00:00:42.515 --> 00:00:51.859
This is the stage that needs judgment: telling “I’ll send the deck Friday” (a commitment) from “someone should look at the deck” (not one, or an unowned item).

9
00:00:52.139 --> 00:01:05.024
Use the structured outputs you built in week 3 so each candidate has the same fields: a title, the owner as mentioned, the due phrase as spoken, a verbatim evidence quote, and the speaker and timestamp.

10
00:01:05.304 --> 00:01:09.165
Ask for the phrase as spoken (“next Friday”), not a resolved date.

11
00:01:09.445 --> 00:01:15.760
The model is not the right place for calendar arithmetic, and keeping the raw phrase makes the next stage checkable.

12
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.661
Verify evidence (code).

13
00:01:17.941 --> 00:01:24.427
For each candidate, confirm the evidence quote occurs in its chunk, after normalizing whitespace and case.

14
00:01:24.707 --> 00:01:31.320
A candidate whose quote does not appear is either a paraphrase or an invention, and either way it is not evidence.

15
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.584
Reject it with a reason and log it.

16
00:01:33.864 --> 00:01:39.048
This one check, which costs nothing, catches a whole class of confident-but-wrong output.

17
00:01:39.328 --> 00:01:41.355
Normalize owners and dates (tools).

18
00:01:41.635 --> 00:01:47.437
Resolve “Marcus” to a directory entry and “next Friday” to a date, measured from the meeting date.

19
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:49.019
Notice the choice here.

20
00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:55.869
If normalization were fully deterministic, you would call these functions directly in code and skip the model.

21
00:01:56.149 --> 00:02:07.221
Tools earn their place when judgment is needed, such as two teammates named Marcus, where the surrounding conversation decides which one was meant, or when the right answer is “unassigned.

22
00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:15.245
” Whichever way you go, the rules from lesson 11 apply: read-only tools, strict schemas, your own checks, instructive errors.

23
00:02:15.525 --> 00:02:17.275
Deduplicate (code first).

24
00:02:17.555 --> 00:02:24.147
The same commitment often appears twice: restated at the end of a meeting, or duplicated by chunk overlap.

25
00:02:24.427 --> 00:02:34.901
Start with a deterministic key (same owner, same due date, highly similar title) and send only the ambiguous pairs to a model, which keeps that call small and rare.

26
00:02:35.181 --> 00:02:36.675
Human review, then write.

27
00:02:36.955 --> 00:02:43.995
Show the reviewer each item with its evidence quote, the resolved owner and date, and anything the pipeline was unsure of.

28
00:02:44.275 --> 00:02:46.920
Approve, edit, or reject in one pass.

29
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.552
Only approved items go to the tracker, through a gated, idempotent write.

30
00:02:51.832 --> 00:02:59.512
This is the single meaningful approval lesson 11 argued for, and the reviewer’s decisions are labeled data you will use later.
